WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.180
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.450
Subsurface scattering means that a portion
of incoming light penetrates the surface of

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:14.629
a material. Our skin is a little known, but
nonetheless great example of that, but so

00:00:14.629 --> 00:00:21.189
are plant leaves, marble, milk, or snails,
to have a wackier example.

00:00:21.189 --> 00:00:26.329
Subsurface scattering looks unbelievably beautiful,
but at the same time, it is very expensive

00:00:26.329 --> 00:00:31.039
to compute because we have to simulate up
to thousands and thousands of light scattering

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:36.460
events for every ray of light. And we have
to do this for millions of rays. It really

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:37.930
takes forever.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:43.239
The lack of subsurface scattering is the reason
why we've seen so many lifeless, rubber-looking

00:00:43.239 --> 00:00:48.090
human characters in video games and animated
movies for decades now.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:52.870
This technique is a collaboration between
the Activision Blizzard game development company,

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:58.250
the University of Zaragoza in Spain, and the
Technical University of Vienna in Austria.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:05.090
And, it can simulate this kind of subsurface
light transport in half a millisecond per image.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:09.550
Let's stop for a minute and think about this.
Earlier, we talked about subsurface scattering

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:14.970
techniques that were really awesome, but still
took at least let's say four hours on a scene

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:22.510
before they became useful. This one is half
a millisecond per image. Almost nothing. In

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:26.660
one second, it can do this calculation two
thousand times.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:31.400
Now, this has to be a completely different
approach than just simulating many millions

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:37.170
of rays of light, right? We can't take a four
hour long algorithm, do some magic and get

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:38.780
something like this.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:43.170
The first key thought is that we can set up
some cool experiment where we play around

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:49.280
with light sources and big blocks of translucent
materials, and record how light bounces off

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:55.670
of these materials. Cool thing number one:
we only need to do it once per material. Number

00:01:55.670 --> 00:02:02.130
two: the results can be stored in an image.
This is what we call a diffusion profile and

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.710
this is how it looks like.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:08.140
So we have an image of the diffusion profile,
and one image of the material that we would

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:10.909
like to add subsurface scattering to.

00:02:10.909 --> 00:02:15.409
This is a convolution-based technique, which
means that it enables us not to add these

00:02:15.409 --> 00:02:20.930
two images together, but to mix them together
in a way that the optical properties of the

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:26.909
diffusion profiles are carried to the image.
If we add the optical properties of an apple

00:02:26.909 --> 00:02:32.739
to a human face, it will look more like a
face that has been carved out of a giant apple.

00:02:32.739 --> 00:02:38.230
A less asinine application is, of course,
if we mix it with the appropriate skin profile

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:43.299
image, then we'll get photorealistic looking
faces, as it is demonstrated quite aptly by

00:02:43.299 --> 00:02:48.700
this animation. This apple to skin example,
by the way, you can actually try for yourself,

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:53.989
as the source code and an executable demo
is also freely available for everyone to experiment

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:54.929
with.

00:02:54.929 --> 00:03:00.060
Convolutions have so many cool applications,
I don't even know where to start. In fact,

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:05.840
I think we should have an episode solely on
that. Can't wait, it's going to be a lot of fun!

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.180
These convolution computations are great,
but they are still too expensive for real-time

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:14.590
video games. What this work gives us, is a
set of techniques that are able to compute

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:20.590
this convolution not on these original images,
but much smaller, tiny-tiny strips which are

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:25.560
much cheaper, but the result of the computations
look barely distinguishable.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.629
Another cool thing is that the quality of
the results is not only scientifically provable,

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:35.829
but this technique also opens up the possibility
of artistic manipulation. It is done in a

00:03:35.829 --> 00:03:41.019
way that we can start out with a physically
plausible result and tailor it to our liking.

00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:44.379
You can see some exaggerated examples of that.

00:03:44.379 --> 00:03:50.659
The entire technique is so simple, a computer
program that executes it can fit on your business card.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:57.139
It also seems to have appeared in Blender
recently. Also, a big hello and a shoutout

00:03:57.139 --> 00:04:01.760
for the awesome people at Intel who recently
invited my humble self to chat a bit about

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.989
this technique. If you would like to hear
more about the details on how this algorithm

00:04:05.989 --> 00:04:08.489
works, I have put some videos in the description
box.

00:04:08.489 --> 00:04:13.779
The most important take home message
from this project, at least for me, is that

00:04:13.779 --> 00:04:18.630
it is possible to conduct academic research
projects together with companies, and create

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:23.730
results that can make it to multi-million
dollar computer games, but also having proven

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:27.010
results that are useful for the scientific
community.

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:31.450
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

